    "final_answer": "I have successfully found the relevant information. Let me provide a comprehensive answer based on what I found.\n\n## Modal Verbs for Expressing Possibility - Cambridge Dictionary Grammar Section\n\nI found this information on the Cambridge Dictionary's Grammar section, specifically on the page **\"Modality: meanings and uses\"** (URL: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses)\n\n> ⚠️ **Note**: The Cambridge Dictionary website was protected by Cloudflare verification during my session, so I accessed the same page content through the Internet Archive's Wayback Machine to retrieve the official Cambridge Grammar content.\n\n### Key Information Found:\n\n**Introduction:**\nCambridge's Grammar section explains that modal verbs express \"an opinion or attitude about a possible fact or to control a possible action.\" Modal words and expressions fall into two main types of meaning:\n1. **Degrees of certainty, possibility, likelihood, doubt**\n2. **Permission, obligation, necessity**\n\n### Modal Verbs for Expressing Possibility (Table from Cambridge):\n\n| Meaning | Modal Verb | Example Sentence |\n|---------|------------|------------------|\n| **possible** | **may** | She **may** be a friend of Richard's. |\n| **possible** | **might** | She **might** be a friend of Richard's. |\n| **possible** | **could** | She **could** be a friend of Richard's. |\n\n### Additional Example Sentences from the Page:\n\n**Expressing possibility (less certain):**\n- \"I **may** go. I haven't decided yet.\"\n\n**Expressing certainty about something impossible:**\n- \"Paula **can't** be home yet. It's impossible. She left 10 minutes after us.\"\n\n**Expressing logical deduction/prediction:**\n- \"There's the phone. That**'ll** be Mum.\"\n- \"You **can't** be hungry. You had a huge lunch.\"\n- \"The traffic isn't heavy. We **should** be there in an hour.\"\n\n### Summary:\n\nThe three modal verbs you specifically asked about — **may**, **might**, and **could** — are all categorized together by Cambridge as expressing **possibility**. The example sentence \"She may/might/could be a friend of Richard's\" is used by Cambridge to demonstrate how each of these modal verbs can be used to express the same idea of possibility, though they differ in their degree of certainty and formality (with \"may\" being more formal than \"might\").",
